------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Presentations of groups
--
-- Amy, Ross and Wesley's presentations R₁⁺ and R₂⁺ as circuits
-- (Circuit.Base): the gates X, CX, K, CCX, and NK, CCZ, and H, by the
-- level at which they enter
--
-- The paper's alphabet (Gates, Syntactics) has a letter for each gate
-- on each qubit and orientation (X₀, X₁, X₂, CX₀₁, CX₁₀, CX₀₂, …), most
-- of them defined by the first relations of Figure 4 from X₀, CX₀₁,
-- CCX₁₂ and K₁₂.  Here the gates are X on one wire, CX (control on the
-- upper wire) and K = H ⊗ H on two and CCX (target on the top wire) on
-- three, at every level; NK = K_[0,1,2,3] (H ⊗ H on the lower wires when
-- the top wire is 0) and CCZ on three from level 1, and H on one from
-- level 2.  Qubit 2, the last tensor factor, is wire 0, and qubit 0 is
-- wire 2.  Each of the paper's words is a circuit of the same name: the
-- gates placed by _↑ (X0 = X ↑ ↑, CX01 = CX ↑, …), and the letters
-- Figure 4 defines by their definitions, which therefore hold by
-- definition and are not rules here.  The other relations are rules,
-- word for word, except the four commutations of gates on disjoint
-- wires ((26), (27), (102), (103)), which are structural; and at level
-- 2 one rule is added: H on wire 1 is K H (H₁ = K₁₂ H₂).  As in the
-- paper, each figure is a relation at every level at which its gates
-- exist (Level, Level₁, Level₂), and Σ₁ and Σ₂ assemble R₁⁺ = Figure 4
-- ∪ Figure 7 ∪ {r_K} and R₂⁺ = R₁⁺ ∪ Figure 8.  Circuits.Iso1 and
-- Circuits.Iso2 show them isomorphic to the paper's on three wires, and
-- Circuits.Presentation carries Theorems 5.2 and 6.3 over.
--
-- Generated by gen/gen_circuits.py from Syntactics.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

